    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An array of length `n` has indexes `0` to `n - 1`. Writing `<=` in the loop condition visits index `n`, which doesn't exist. What happens next depends on the language — and none of the outcomes are good:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**JavaScript** quietly returns `undefined`, and the bug surfaces later as `NaN` or a strange comparison.",
        "**Python** and **Java** crash with an `IndexError` / `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` — loud, but at least honest.",
        "**C++** reads whatever memory happens to be next. The program may seem to work, then fail somewhere else entirely."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Habit:** when you write a loop bound or an index like `i + 1`, ask \"what happens on the last element?\" If you read `nums[i + 1]`, the loop must stop at `i < n - 1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Forgetting the empty array"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`nums[0]` on an empty array is the same out-of-bounds read. Code like `let max = nums[0]` assumes at least one element. Either check first, or confirm the problem guarantees `n ≥ 1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function maxOrNull(nums) {\n  if (nums.length === 0) return null;   // handle the edge case first\n  let max = nums[0];\n  for (const x of nums) max = Math.max(max, x);\n  return max;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Two names, one array (aliasing)"
    },
