    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In JavaScript, Python and Java, an array variable holds a **reference** to the array, not a copy of it. So `b = a` gives you a second name for the **same** array:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const a = [1, 2, 3];\nconst b = a;          // same array!\nb[0] = 99;\nconsole.log(a);       // [99, 2, 3]\n\nconst copy = [...a];  // a real copy (also: a.slice())"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a                 # same list!\nb[0] = 99\nprint(a)              # [99, 2, 3]\n\ncopy = a[:]           # a real copy (also: list(a), a.copy())"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] a = {1, 2, 3};\nint[] b = a;                      // same array!\nb[0] = 99;\nSystem.out.println(a[0]);         // 99\n\nint[] copy = a.clone();           // a real copy (also: Arrays.copyOf)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> a = {1, 2, 3};\nvector<int> b = a;        // C++ COPIES a vector on assignment\nb[0] = 99;\ncout << a[0] << endl;     // 1 — a is unchanged\n\nvector<int>& alias = a;   // a reference makes a second name instead"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "C++ is the odd one out: assigning a `vector` copies it, and you ask for sharing explicitly with `&`. The same rule catches people in function calls — passing a `vector` by value copies it, which is slow for big arrays."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "4. Changing an array while looping over it"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Removing elements during a forward loop shifts the rest of the array left, so the loop skips the element that slid into the current position:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const nums = [2, 4, 4, 5];\nfor (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n  if (nums[i] === 4) nums.splice(i, 1);   // ✗ the second 4 slides into i and is skipped\n}\nconsole.log(nums);                          // [2, 4, 5]\n\nconst kept = nums.filter((x) => x !== 4);  // ✓ build a new array instead"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Fixes: build a new array, loop **backwards** when deleting, or use the read/write pointer pattern from **Move zeroes**."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5. Integer overflow in sums and products"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In Java and C++, an `int` holds values up to about 2.1 billion. Summing 100,000 values of up to 100,000 each gives 10 billion — silently wrong in an `int`. Use `long` (Java) or `long long` (C++) for sums and products. The classic middle-index calculation has the same trap:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "int mid = (low + high) / 2;          // can overflow when low + high > 2^31 - 1\nint mid = low + (high - low) / 2;    // safe"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "6. Language gotchas worth memorising"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**JavaScript's default sort compares text:** `[10, 9, 1].sort()` gives `[1, 10, 9]`. Always pass a comparator for numbers: `sort((a, b) => a - b)`.",
        "**Python's `[[0] * cols] * rows`** creates `rows` references to the **same** row — changing one cell changes a whole column. Use `[[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]`.",
        "**Comparing arrays with `==`** compares references in JavaScript and Java, not contents. Use a loop, `Arrays.equals` in Java, or `==` on lists in Python (which does compare contents).",
        "**C++ `size()` is unsigned:** `for (int i = 0; i < v.size() - 1; i++)` misbehaves when `v` is empty, because `0 - 1` wraps to a huge number."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A 20-second checklist before you run your code"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Loop bounds: `<` or `<=`? Any `i + 1` or `i - 1` reads?",
        "What if the array is **empty**? What if it has **one** element?",
        "Did I copy the array, or just make a second name for it?",
        "Am I changing the array I'm looping over?",
        "Could a sum or product overflow?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "You'll find these checks repeated as **Edge cases to test** at the end of every problem in this course."
    }
  ],
